e 347, Issue 11\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\n### Published by\n\nShow more\n\nResearch article\n\nGet rights and content\n\n# The seating couples problem in the even case\n\nAuthor links open overlay panelMariusz Meszka ^{a}, Anita Pasotti ^{b}, Marco Antonio Pellegrini ^{c}\n\n\n10.1016/j.disc.2024.114182\n\nAccess through your organizationPurchase PDF\n\nMore actions\n\n  * Article preview\n  * Recommended articles\n  * Cited by (2)\n  * Metrics\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this paper we consider the seating couples problem with an even number of seats, which, using graph theory terminology, can be stated as follows. Given a positive even integer and a list L containing n positive integers not exceeding n, is it always possible to find a perfect matching of {K}_{v} whose list of edge-lengths is L? Up to now a (non-constructive) solution is known only when all the edge-lengths are coprime with v. In this paper we firstly present some necessary conditions for the existence of a solution. Then, we give a complete constructive solution when the list consists of one or two distinct elements, and when the list consists of consecutive integers , each one appearing with the same multiplicity. Finally, we propose a conjecture and some open problems.\n\n## Keywords\n\nSeating couples problem\n\n;\n\nMatching\n\n;\n\nSkolem sequence\n\n\n## Introduction\n\nIn [8] the authors considered the following question proposed by Roland Bacher and called the “king's problem” or the “seating couples problem”. A king invites n couples for dinner at his round table containing seats, the king taking the last unoccupied chair. The king has to address the following task: given an arbitrary set of n couples, no one married for more than n years, is it always possible to seat all n couples at his table according to the royal protocol stipulating that, if the two spouses of a couple are in their i-th year of marriage, they have to occupy two chairs at circular distance i (where circular distance i means that the two chairs are separated by exactly chairs)? \nGiven a graph Γ, we denote by and its vertex-set and its edge-set, respectively. If Γ has an odd (even, respectively) number of vertices, say (2n, resp.), a near 1-factor (a 1-factor, resp.) of Γ is a spanning subgraph of Γ with n pairwise disjoint edges. In the even case, one also speaks of a perfect matching of Γ.\n\nBacher proposed the following, see [8].\n\nConjecture 1\n\nThere exists a solution to the seating couples problem if all distances, namely all edge-lengths, are invertible elements modulo the total number of seats, namely the number of the vertices of the complete graph.\n\nThis conjecture has been generalized in [7] as follows.\n\nConjecture 2\n\nLet be an odd integer and L be a list of n positive integers not exceeding n. Then there exists a near 1-factor F of {K}_{v} such that if and only if the following condition holds:\\mathit{\\text{for any divisor }}d\\mathit{\\text{ of }}v\\mathit{\\text{, the number of multiples of }}d\\mathit{\\text{ appearing in }}L\\mathit{\\text{ does not exceed }}\\frac{v-d}{2}\\mathit{\\text{.}}\n\nWith the aid of a computer, the authors of [7] verified its validity for all odd integers and pointed out that in the statement, the actual conjecture is the sufficiency. In fact, they proved that Condition (1.1) is necessary. Furthermore, their main result is:\n\nTheorem 1.1\n\nLet L be a list of n positive integers not exceeding n and let . Then, Conjecture 2 holds whenever the underlying set S of L satisfies one of the following conditions:\n\n  * (1)\n\nor n;\n\n  * (2)\n\n, where is not coprime with v.\n\nFurthermore, Conjecture 2 holds if with . Finally, if and , they also presented some partial results for the cases not covered by Theorem 1.1. In particular, they gave a complete solution for .\n\nThe case odd prime has been considered in [8], in which the authors proved the existence of a complete solution by a nonconstructive proof.\n\nTheorem 1.2\n\nThere exists a solution to the seating couples problem with p seats for every odd prime p.\n\nAn alternative (not constructive) proof can be found in [3], based on the famous Combinatorial Nullstellensatz [1]. Partial constructive results in the prime case can be found in [9]. We underline that Marco Buratti [2] proposed a strongly related conjecture, for details see [6] and the references therein. Clearly, one can consider the same problem also in the even case. In fact, in [4] the authors proved the following:\n\nTheorem 1.3\n\nLet be a positive integer and let L be a list of n integers coprime with 2n not exceeding n. Then there exists a perfect matching F of {K}_{v} such that .\n\nWe point out that this result was conjectured by Michal Adamaszek (private communication) and that, when n is a prime, Theorem 1.3 was already proved by Tamás R. Mezei in his Master's Thesis [5]. Clearly, the condition that all the edge-lengths are coprime with 2n is not necessary. Take for instance and ; then, is a perfect matching of {K}_{4} such that . This trivial example also shows that in the even case Condition (1.1) of Conjecture 2 is not necessary.\n\nIn this paper we consider the seating couples problem in the even case removing the hypothesis that all the elements in the list are coprime with the order of the complete graph. Firstly, in Section 2, we present some necessary conditions for the existence of a perfect matching of the complete graph having a given list of edge-lengths and we give results for some special classes of lists, including the case in which the underlying set has size one.\n\nThen, it is natural to consider lists having exactly two distinct edge-lengths, see Section 3. In this case, we obtain a full classification, as described in the following.\n\nTheorem 1.4\n\nLet be four integers such that , and . Let , and . There exists a perfect matching F of {K}_{2n} such that if and only if d divides n and one of the following cases occurs:\n\n  * (1)\n\n\\frac{x}{d}